    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hi, i'm working on my sociology thesis and i'm getting a bit stuck on the interpretation. i ran a t-test on my survey data regarding social media usage and community engagement. my p-value came out as 0.042 and the 95% confidence interval for the difference in means is [0.12, 0.85]. could you explain how i should technically interpret these two specific values in my results section to show statistical significance?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A p-value of 0.042, which is less than the conventional alpha level of 0.05, indicates that you can reject the null hypothesis. This means there is a statistically significant difference in social media usage and community engagement between your groups. The 95% confidence interval of [0.12, 0.85] tells you that you are 95% confident that the true difference in means lies somewhere between 0.12 and 0.85. Essentially, since the entire interval is above zero, it supports the finding of a significant difference.",
